# HelloVerify — environment contract
#
# Copy to `.env.local` for development. Nothing in this file is a secret; it is
# the list of names the application reads and what happens when they are absent.
#
# THE RULE (BUILD-SPEC §10, AUDIT E1): no credential is ever prefixed
# NEXT_PUBLIC_. Anything so prefixed is inlined into the JavaScript bundle and
# is public by construction — that is precisely how the previous build shipped
# the CRM tokens and an AES key to every visitor. The lead pipeline reads these
# only from `src/lib/leads/*` and `src/lib/integrations/*`, both of which carry
# `import "server-only"`, so a client import fails the build.


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Zoho CRM — REST v8 (server-only). NEVER prefix any of these NEXT_PUBLIC_.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Why REST and not the Web-to-Lead endpoint the old site used: Web-to-Lead
# tokens are public by design — Zoho publishes them in the webform's embed
# source — so relocating them to the server does not make them secret, and
# anyone who loaded the old site still holds the pair. The old client also
# posted with `mode: 'no-cors'`, making every response opaque: it could not
# tell a delivered lead from a dropped one. A refresh token is a real secret
# and REST reports per record whether Zoho kept it.
#
# Obtain once, from a Self Client in the Zoho API console
# (https://api-console.zoho.com) with scope ZohoCRM.modules.leads.CREATE and
# ZohoCRM.settings.fields.READ:
ZOHO_CLIENT_ID=
ZOHO_CLIENT_SECRET=
ZOHO_REFRESH_TOKEN=

# Data centre for the ACCOUNTS host only. The API host is not set here — the
# refresh response returns `api_domain` and the bridge uses that, so an org in
# another region cannot end up 401ing against a hardcoded host.
#   .com (US, what the old site used) | .in | .eu | .com.au | .jp | .ca | .sa
ZOHO_ACCOUNTS_HOST=https://accounts.zoho.com

# OPTIONAL, and the one value that used to lose leads. Lead_Source is a
# picklist; under Web-to-Lead an unrecognised value made Zoho answer HTTP 200
# and silently drop the record ("HelloVerify" did exactly that). REST reports
# the rejection instead, and on first send the bridge checks this against the
# live picklist and logs at ERROR if it is not there.
#
# Leave UNSET to omit the field entirely, which is what the old site does
# today. Set it only to a value that appears verbatim in
# Setup > Leads > Fields > Lead Source.
# ZOHO_LEAD_SOURCE=Web Form


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Server Actions on Cloud Run
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Next encrypts the variables a Server Action closes over, using a key derived
# per build unless one is supplied. Cloud Run runs more than one container as
# soon as it scales, and an action reference encrypted by instance A must be
# decryptable by instance B, so this MUST be set and MUST be identical across
# every instance of a given deployment. Without it, submissions fail on
# whichever container did not render the page.
#
# Generate once per environment, then treat it as a secret:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
NEXT_SERVER_ACTIONS_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# NOT an environment variable, but the other half of the same story, recorded
# here because it fails in production and never in development:
#
# Next rejects an action POST whose Origin does not match Host or
# X-Forwarded-Host. Behind the external HTTPS load balancer this works only if
# the original Host survives the hop. If the LB rewrites it, every submission
# returns a CSRF failure and the fix is `serverActions.allowedOrigins` in
# next.config.ts. Left unset deliberately: guessing the production hostname
# here would either do nothing or widen the check for no reason. Verify against
# the real load balancer before cutover.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Lead pipeline — abuse protection (BUILD-SPEC §10)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Submissions allowed per identity per window. §10 calls five per ten minutes a
# generous ceiling for a form a real person submits once.
LEADS_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=5
LEADS_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS=600000

# Minimum plausible time to fill the form, in milliseconds. Anything faster is
# treated as automated. See the honesty note in src/lib/leads/schema.ts about
# what this check is worth on a statically prerendered page.
LEADS_MIN_FILL_MS=2500

# How many proxy hops to trust at the right-hand end of X-Forwarded-For.
# Behind a GCP external HTTPS load balancer the header ends with
# "<client ip>, <forwarding rule ip>", so the client is the second from the
# right and the correct value is 2. Getting this wrong in either direction is a
# real bug: too low and every user shares one rate-limit bucket, too high and
# the client can spoof its own identity by sending its own XFF header.
LEADS_TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=2

# Set to 0 to skip the MX lookup (useful offline; the disposable-domain list
# still applies). The lookup fails open on DNS errors — a DNS hiccup must not
# cost a real enquiry.
LEADS_MX_CHECK=1

# Shown to the user when a lead validates but cannot be delivered. Must be a
# monitored address: this is the only route left at that point.
LEADS_FALLBACK_EMAIL=sales@helloverify.com


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Analytics — GA4 (BUILD-SPEC §11a.5, TASKS.md Part 10)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# THIS ONE IS NEXT_PUBLIC_ AND THAT IS NOT A VIOLATION OF THE RULE ABOVE. A GA4
# measurement ID is public by design — it ships in the tag on every page of
# every site that uses GA, and it authorises nothing. The rule exists for
# credentials; this is an identifier.
#
# UNSET IS A REAL STATE, not a missing value. With no id:
#   - components/analytics/Analytics.tsx renders nothing: no script, no
#     request, no cookie
#   - next.config.ts does NOT add Google's origins to script-src, connect-src
#     or img-src, so the policy stays as strict as it is today
#   - §9.4's measured zero third-party origins stays zero
# Measured both ways: with an id the header gains googletagmanager and the four
# analytics hosts and the homepage grows 2.4 KB; without it neither the header
# nor the body mentions Google at all.
#
# TWO THINGS THAT ARE NOT THIS VARIABLE, and setting it does not supply them:
#
#   1. /legal/cookie-policy is a stub. Every section reads "[ Section text
#      pending legal review ]", including "Analytics and performance". Setting
#      an analytics cookie while the policy says nothing about it is not
#      defensible, and the Organization node's areaServed includes the UK.
#   2. There is no consent UI, so Consent Mode initialises with
#      analytics_storage denied. GA4 then sends cookieless pings and stores
#      nothing until something calls gtag('consent','update',…). Nothing does.
#      Until a banner exists the reports are modelled rather than counted, so
#      the AI-referral numbers will be directional.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GA_MEASUREMENT_ID=G-XXXXXXXXXX
